1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,452
A phone charges in the
day and runs all evening.

2
00:00:03,452 --> 00:00:05,928
Flour and sugar sit ready in a kitchen.

3
00:00:05,928 --> 00:00:08,976
A box of building blocks
waits to become a house.

4
00:00:09,376 --> 00:00:11,959
First you get the energy and the parts.

5
00:00:11,959 --> 00:00:13,097
Then you build.

6
00:00:13,547 --> 00:00:16,328
Diya learned that a leaf
makes ATP and NADPH in light.

7
00:00:16,328 --> 00:00:18,108
She asked: where are they spent?

8
00:00:18,108 --> 00:00:20,843
And how does carbon dioxide
from the air become sugar?

9
00:00:21,193 --> 00:00:22,690
Here is the short answer.

10
00:00:22,690 --> 00:00:26,426
In the stroma, a cycle of reactions
spends ATP and NADPH to fix carbon

11
00:00:26,426 --> 00:00:27,574
dioxide into sugar.

12
00:00:27,574 --> 00:00:28,989
It is the Calvin cycle.

13
00:00:29,489 --> 00:00:33,477
By the end, you will know RuBP,
RuBisCO, the three stages, and how much

14
00:00:33,477 --> 00:00:35,298
ATP and NADPH one glucose costs.

15
00:00:35,948 --> 00:00:37,644
First, who found this cycle?

16
00:00:37,994 --> 00:00:41,956
Melvin Calvin fed algae with carbon
dioxide made of radioactive carbon

17
00:00:41,956 --> 00:00:42,856
fourteen.

18
00:00:42,671 --> 00:00:45,137
Then he tracked where that carbon went.

19
00:00:45,587 --> 00:00:49,411
The first stable product was a three
carbon acid, three phosphoglyceric

20
00:00:49,411 --> 00:00:50,445
acid, or PGA.

21
00:00:50,445 --> 00:00:53,515
Plants that make PGA first
are called C three plants.

22
00:00:54,165 --> 00:00:56,349
Let's zoom in to where this happens.

23
00:00:56,700 --> 00:00:59,426
In the leaf, each mesophyll
cell holds many chloroplasts.

24
00:00:59,926 --> 00:01:01,842
Zoom into one chloroplast.

25
00:01:01,842 --> 00:01:05,973
The cycle runs in the stroma,
the fluid around the grana.

26
00:01:06,473 --> 00:01:08,167
Zoom into the stroma.

27
00:01:08,167 --> 00:01:13,371
Here an enzyme called RuBisCO joins
carbon dioxide to a five carbon sugar.

28
00:01:14,021 --> 00:01:16,812
Now run the Calvin cycle, stage by stage.

29
00:01:17,162 --> 00:01:18,702
Stage one, carboxylation.

30
00:01:18,702 --> 00:01:22,637
Carbon dioxide joins RuBP, ribulose
bisphosphate, a five carbon sugar.

31
00:01:22,637 --> 00:01:23,787
RuBisCO does this.

32
00:01:24,537 --> 00:01:26,443
Five carbons plus one makes six.

33
00:01:26,443 --> 00:01:30,520
This splits at once into two molecules
of three PGA, three carbons each.

34
00:01:31,270 --> 00:01:32,519
Stage two, reduction.

35
00:01:32,519 --> 00:01:33,902
ATP and NADPH are spent.

36
00:01:33,902 --> 00:01:36,502
Per carbon dioxide fixed,
two ATP and two NADPH.

37
00:01:36,502 --> 00:01:37,513
This makes sugar.

38
00:01:38,263 --> 00:01:39,728
Stage three, regeneration.

39
00:01:39,728 --> 00:01:42,779
One more ATP rebuilds RuBP,
so the cycle can turn again.

40
00:01:43,529 --> 00:01:46,380
So each carbon dioxide costs
three ATP and two NADPH.

41
00:01:47,130 --> 00:01:48,030
Remember this.

42
00:01:48,005 --> 00:01:49,265
The acceptor is RuBP.

43
00:01:49,865 --> 00:01:52,104
Finding that acceptor was not easy.

44
00:01:52,454 --> 00:01:55,887
For many years, scientists
searched for a two carbon acceptor.

45
00:01:55,887 --> 00:01:59,116
Then they found it was a five
carbon ketose sugar, RuBP.

46
00:01:59,566 --> 00:02:02,392
RuBisCO's full name is
RuBP carboxylase oxygenase.

47
00:02:02,392 --> 00:02:03,976
It can also bind oxygen.

48
00:02:03,976 --> 00:02:06,729
It is the most plentiful
enzyme in the world.

49
00:02:07,379 --> 00:02:09,339
Now compare two kinds of plants.

50
00:02:09,689 --> 00:02:10,817
C three plants.

51
00:02:10,817 --> 00:02:14,259
The first stable product is
PGA, a three carbon acid.

52
00:02:14,709 --> 00:02:15,683
C four plants.

53
00:02:15,683 --> 00:02:19,356
The first product is oxaloacetic
acid, OAA, a four carbon acid.

54
00:02:19,356 --> 00:02:21,934
But even C four plants
run the Calvin cycle.

55
00:02:22,584 --> 00:02:24,382
Is this a dark reaction?

56
00:02:24,732 --> 00:02:26,479
It does not need light directly.

57
00:02:26,479 --> 00:02:29,167
But it needs ATP and NADPH
from the light reaction.

58
00:02:29,617 --> 00:02:33,762
So it goes on for a short while after
the light is switched off, and then

59
00:02:33,762 --> 00:02:34,662
stops.

60
00:02:34,293 --> 00:02:36,801
Switch the light on, and it starts again.

61
00:02:37,401 --> 00:02:38,301
Your turn.

62
00:02:38,167 --> 00:02:42,070
In C three plants, what is the
first stable product: PGA or OAA?

63
00:02:42,070 --> 00:02:43,197
Pause and decide.

64
00:02:46,397 --> 00:02:47,297
PGA!

65
00:02:46,779 --> 00:02:47,893
A three carbon acid.

66
00:02:48,543 --> 00:02:50,140
Now, a worked example.

67
00:02:50,140 --> 00:02:52,180
How much does one glucose cost?

68
00:02:52,530 --> 00:02:54,463
Glucose has six carbons.

69
00:02:54,463 --> 00:02:57,106
One turn fixes one carbon dioxide.

70
00:02:57,106 --> 00:02:58,936
So we need six turns.

71
00:02:59,386 --> 00:03:01,621
Each turn costs three ATP and two NADPH.

72
00:03:01,621 --> 00:03:03,431
Six times three is eighteen ATP.

73
00:03:03,431 --> 00:03:05,158
Six times two is twelve NADPH.

74
00:03:05,608 --> 00:03:08,843
In: six carbon dioxide,
eighteen ATP, twelve NADPH.

75
00:03:08,843 --> 00:03:11,706
Out: one glucose,
eighteen ADP, twelve NADP.

76
00:03:12,356 --> 00:03:13,818
Now, the exam trap.

77
00:03:14,168 --> 00:03:17,722
Many students think the Calvin cycle
happens only in C three plants.

78
00:03:18,122 --> 00:03:22,585
But it runs in all photosynthetic
plants, C three or C four.

79
00:03:23,235 --> 00:03:24,437
Where is this asked?

80
00:03:24,437 --> 00:03:28,215
As number questions on ATP and NADPH,
and statements on RuBP, RuBisCO and

81
00:03:28,215 --> 00:03:29,115
PGA.

82
00:03:29,226 --> 00:03:30,376
Exam level now.

83
00:03:30,376 --> 00:03:32,071
Read the four statements.

84
00:03:32,071 --> 00:03:33,269
Pause and solve.

85
00:03:38,669 --> 00:03:39,943
The answer is B.

86
00:03:39,943 --> 00:03:42,372
Statements one and three are correct.

87
00:03:42,872 --> 00:03:47,984
RuBP is a five carbon sugar, and the
cycle runs in all photosynthetic plants.

88
00:03:48,484 --> 00:03:51,525
But one glucose needs
eighteen ATP, not twelve.

89
00:03:51,525 --> 00:03:53,973
And regeneration uses ATP, not NADPH.

90
00:03:54,573 --> 00:03:56,114
Here is your recap.

91
00:03:56,114 --> 00:03:57,698
Screenshot this card.

92
00:04:00,898 --> 00:04:03,084
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

93
00:04:03,484 --> 00:04:04,384
One.

94
00:04:03,959 --> 00:04:05,396
What does RuBisCO do?

95
00:04:06,296 --> 00:04:07,196
Two.

96
00:04:06,691 --> 00:04:08,887
Name the three stages of the Calvin cycle.

97
00:04:09,787 --> 00:04:10,687
Three.

98
00:04:10,213 --> 00:04:12,119
How many ATP and NADPH make one glucose?

99
00:04:13,219 --> 00:04:18,252
Next time: why do maize and sugarcane
fix carbon dioxide twice, and why

100
00:04:18,252 --> 00:04:21,516
does RuBisCO sometimes
grab oxygen by mistake?

101
00:04:21,516 --> 00:04:25,472
We find out in the C four
pathway and photorespiration.
